WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.101
Safe dynamic allocation starts with choosing the simplest ownership model that fits.

2
00:00:05.101 --> 00:00:06.802
Prefer values when possible.

3
00:00:06.802 --> 00:00:09.353
Prefer standard containers for dynamic sequences.

4
00:00:09.353 --> 00:00:12.754
Use RAII to tie cleanup to object lifetime.

5
00:00:12.754 --> 00:00:19.556
Use smart pointers when dynamic ownership is necessary and the ownership relationship needs to be explicit.

6
00:00:19.556 --> 00:00:22.106
Validate sizes before allocating or constructing.

7
00:00:22.106 --> 00:00:28.908
External counts, lengths, dimensions, and element sizes should be checked against domain limits and arithmetic boundaries.

8
00:00:28.908 --> 00:00:38.261
Objects should be initialized before they are exposed, and partially constructed resources should remain under the control of the owner that can

9
00:00:38.261 --> 00:00:39.537
clean them up.

10
00:00:39.537 --> 00:00:42.512
Define cleanup, failure, and resource-limit behavior.

11
00:00:42.512 --> 00:00:45.913
Cleanup must work on normal and error paths.

12
00:00:45.913 --> 00:00:48.464
Allocation and deallocation mechanisms must match.

13
00:00:48.464 --> 00:00:51.440
Resource pressure should produce predictable failure behavior.

14
00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:56.116
Large or untrusted requests should meet clear maximums before allocation begins.

15
00:00:56.116 --> 00:00:57.817
Document specialized allocation strategies.

16
00:00:57.817 --> 00:01:07.170
Custom allocators, pools, arenas, and std::pmr designs can be useful, but they need explicit lifetime, reset, exhaustion, and thread-safety rules.

17
00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:11.846
Performance optimization should make lifecycle more intentional, not harder to understand.

18
00:01:11.846 --> 00:01:14.822
Finally, test, review, and revisit allocation assumptions.

19
00:01:14.822 --> 00:01:20.348
Use sanitizers, diagnostics, static analysis, boundary tests, cleanup tests, and lifecycle-focused reviews.

20
00:01:20.348 --> 00:01:27.150
Safe dynamic allocation is not one feature; it is disciplined design across the full object lifecycle.

21
00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.276
The practical goal is consistency.

22
00:01:29.276 --> 00:01:38.204
New code should avoid introducing unclear ownership, and legacy code should move steadily toward types and APIs that express lifecycle directly.

23
00:01:38.204 --> 00:01:42.880
Each improvement should make the next review easier than the last.
